  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1136 PM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nThe synoptic pattern aloft continues to be dominated by a very\nlarge high pressure complex over the southern CONUS and \nresiding overhead of the forecast area. The only other aspect of\nthe patttern currently is a very strong low pressure system \nthat continues to deepen over the Pacific Northwest. Today's \nconvection greatly over preformed and has dropped temperatures \ndown into the 70/80s. Some weak convection continues in very far\nSW Kansas, but CAMs have these storms slowly dying off. \nAdditionally, some CAM's have scattered overnight convection \nacross the CWA that will all remain sub-severe. The best chances\nappear to be up near Hays in the northern counties at around 8 \nAM to noon. \n\nA  heat advisory remains in effect east of US 283 until Sunday \nat 8 PM. Highs Sunday are forecast in the mid-to-upper 90s and \neven exceeding triple digits in the far southeastern portion of \nthe CWA. Other than the heat that continues to blanket the area,\nthe forecast focus is on more storm chances in the evening. \nCAMs have weak storms developing in eastern Colorado around 7 PM\nand moving into Kansas. Progged CAPE and shear are very minimal\nand storms are expected to remain well below severe thresholds \nif the storms even materialize. \n\nMonday and Tuesday are forecast to continue the heat with highs \nin the upper 90s. The next best chance for precipitation comes \nTuesday night with 30-40% storm chances via ensembles in the \nnorthwestern counties. Although it is still early, it is \nexpected that these storms continue to be sub-severe with strong\nwinds up to 50 mph being possible. Wednesday, ensembles have \nanother wave of precipitation, this time more widespread. More \ntime will be needed before making declarative statements on the \nconvection. The rest of the forecast period will remain hot, \nwith sunday even cooling down enough for highs be around 90 \ndegrees. This would easily be the coolest day of the forecast \nperiod. Through this stretch, modest relative humidity minimums \nremain above 25% quelling most of the fire weather risk. This is\nforecast to accompany a continuing south wind at 10-15 mph.",
